-- Forward-only step 7 of 8 in the Production schema upgrade.
--
-- Start: supabase/migrations/20260728213131_remote_schema.sql, the recovered and
--        validated Production baseline.
-- End:   the accepted repository schema.
--
-- Privileges, stated in full rather than diffed.
--
-- The baseline carries ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES ... GRANT ALL ON TABLES, SEQUENCES
-- and FUNCTIONS TO anon and authenticated — stock Supabase, where RLS rather than
-- table privilege is the control. Every object step 01 and step 02 create therefore
-- arrives with anon and authenticated already holding everything, whether or not a
-- migration says so. Leaving that implicit is how a table ends up reachable by a
-- browser role nobody meant to expose, so each object this chain touches is first
-- revoked from PUBLIC, anon and authenticated, and then granted exactly what the
-- accepted repository schema grants it — no more.
--
-- Three tables are deliberately narrower than the default would leave them, matching
-- the repository's own hardening:
--   consumer_briefcase_items  no INSERT or UPDATE for anon or authenticated
--                             (supabase/phase-52-rcap-consumer-payment-authority.sql)
--   partner_entitlement       nothing at all for anon or authenticated
--                             (supabase/phase-43-rcap-partner-onboarding-phase1.sql)
--   rcap_persons              nothing at all for anon or authenticated
--                             (supabase/phase-54-rcap-person-namespace-hardening.sql)
-- Each is written server-side through the service-role client, so no browser-role
-- path loses access it uses.
--
-- Where anon or authenticated do keep access to a table this chain creates, that
-- table has row level security enabled with policies, established in step 06;
-- scripts/verify-rcap-production-schema-upgrade.mjs fails if any such table does not.
--
-- Statements were read out of the built target database rather than written by hand,
-- because column-level grants and the default-privilege interaction above are both
-- easy to get subtly wrong.

